  "worked_solution": "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}+4z & =10 \\\\ 2({z}^{2}+2z) & =10 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-1 & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, $p=1$ and $q=-6.$ \n\nNow, let's solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1)^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ |z+1| & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
